  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Both sit in this site's "Expensive" bracket, but for opposite reasons. <strong>Orange</strong> charges more because you're getting a carrier-grade bundle: unlimited calls, SMS, and hotspot included standard, backed by one of Europe's largest telecom groups. <strong>Ubigi</strong> charges more because it's an established MVNO (part of NTT Group) selling per-GB and recurring subscription plans rather than the cheapest possible data. Neither is priced to compete with a budget data-only eSIM; the question is which kind of "expensive" you're actually paying for.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Calls Bundle or Recurring Data Subscription</h2>
  <p>
    Orange Travel is the international eSIM arm of Orange, and its plans include unlimited calls and SMS plus hotspot tethering as standard, not a paid add-on. Its discounts are automatic strike-through markdowns built into bigger plan tiers, not a code you type in; we checked its checkout flow directly and found no promo code field at all, so the AW0 code once listed here is now marked invalid.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ubigi is a brand of Transatel, a French MVNO enabler now part of NTT Group, and it sells data-only plans in two structures: one-off fixed allowances, or recurring subscriptions that renew automatically. Its monthly plan can't be cancelled until you've held it three months minimum; its annual plan is paid upfront and can't be cancelled or refunded at all during that year. Unlike Orange, Ubigi does have a working discount code, ADAMANDLINDS, and it's ours.
  </p>

