  <h2>Is Orange or Ubigi the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Orange if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You specifically want calls and SMS included standard, not as a separate purchase</li>
        <li>You're booking a longer stay (up to 90 days) where the bigger automatic markdowns kick in</li>
        <li>You'd rather buy from an established carrier brand than a smaller reseller</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Ubigi if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You just want data, not a calls/SMS bundle you won't use</li>
        <li>You return to the same country regularly enough that a recurring monthly plan makes sense</li>
        <li>You want a working discount code today rather than a fixed automatic markdown</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Orange's bundle only pays for itself if you'd otherwise be paying for calls and texts anyway.</strong> If WhatsApp or a similar app covers your calling and texting while traveling, as it does for most people, you're paying Orange's premium for a bundle you won't use, and a data-only plan from Ubigi (or a cheaper per-GB provider like <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a>) will usually beat it on price for the data alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ubigi's recurring plans lock you in a way Orange's don't.</strong> Orange sells fixed-term plans with no auto-renewal to worry about. Ubigi's monthly subscription can't be cancelled for three months, and its annual plan can't be cancelled or refunded at all during its 12-month term, something worth reading before you pick a recurring option over a one-off.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Orange vs Ubigi FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Orange or Ubigi</h3>
      <p>Ubigi, for data alone, outside Orange's home region of Europe. Orange's higher price includes unlimited calls, SMS, and hotspot as standard, which Ubigi doesn't offer at all; if you only need data, that bundle isn't buying you anything.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either have a working discount code</h3>
      <p>Ubigi does: ADAMANDLINDS gives 10% off any plan, and it's our own code. Orange doesn't; its previously listed code, AW0, doesn't appear on its real checkout and is marked invalid. Its discounts instead come as automatic markdowns on bigger plan tiers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is better if I need calls and texts, not just data</h3>
      <p>Orange, without question. Its Europe plans bundle unlimited calls and SMS in as standard. Ubigi is data-only across every plan it sells.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is easier to cancel</h3>
      <p>Orange, since its plans are fixed-term with nothing that auto-renews. Ubigi's monthly subscription requires a 3-month minimum commitment before cancellation, and its annual plan can't be cancelled or refunded during its 12-month term at all.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

